{"id":"9bd5d648-27f9-470b-b3c8-af383a986ff5","object":"chat.completion","created":1784961967,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    nodes = case['nodes']\\n    required_symbols = set(case['required_symbols'])\\n    \\n    # Build node map and children\\n    node_map = {}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        node_map[nid] = {**node, 'children': []}\\n    for node in nodes:\\n        parent = node.get('parent')\\n        if parent is not None:\\n            node_map[parent]['children'].append(node['id'])\\n    \\n    # Precompute depth? Already present but we also need ordering\\n    forced_retain = set()\\n    symbol_counts = {sym: 0 for sym in required_symbols}\\n    for nid, node in node_map.items():\\n        if not node['removable'] or node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            forced_retain.add(nid)\\n        if node['symbol'] in required_symbols:\\n            symbol_counts[node['symbol']] += 1\\n    \\n    # Initially all nodes retained\\n    retained = set(node_map.keys())\\n    removed_order = []\\n    \\n    # Sort by depth descending, then id\\n    order = sorted(node_map.values(), key=lambda x: (-x['depth'], x['id']))\\n    for node in order:\\n        nid = node['id']\\n        if nid not in retained:\\n            continue\\n        if nid in forced_retain:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any immediate child is retained\\n        if any(child in retained for child in node['children']):\\n            continue\\n        # Check required symbol loss\\n        sym = node['symbol']\\n        if sym in required_symbols and symbol_counts[sym] == 1:\\n            continue\\n        # Remove\\n        retained.remove(nid)\\n        removed_order.append(nid)\\n        if sym in required_symbols:\\n            symbol_counts[sym] -= 1\\n    \\n    return {\\n        'removed_node_ids': list(removed_order),\\n        'retained_node_ids': sorted(retained)\\n    }\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) that processes a payload with nodes and required_symbols. The task is \"grammar_guided_node_reduction\" - essentially a tree reduction algorithm similar to Perses. The goal: given a tree of nodes with parent pointers, symbols, depths, removable flags, utility values, and required_symbols, we need to decide which nodes to remove (prune) while preserving that all required symbols are still present in the remaining tree. The root is always retained (not removable).\n\nThe algorithm described: Consider deeper nodes before shallower (i.e., process nodes in descending depth). For each removable node, tentatively remove it if it passes: 1) all required symbols remain represented (i.e., after removal, the set of symbols from retained nodes still includes all required_symbols), 2) no retained children? Actually \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node can only be removed if it has no retained children? That seems odd for a tree reduction. Let's re-read: \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So if a node has children that are retained (not removed), we cannot remove that node because it would orphan them? Actually in a tree, if you remove a node, you remove its entire subtree. So the condition might be: a node can be removed only if it has no children that are going to be retained (i.e., all its descendants are also considered for removal). But the algorithm processes deeper nodes first, so children are considered before parents. So when we get to a parent, children may have been removed already. If any child is retained, the parent cannot be removed because that would remove the child. So yes, only if all children are removed (or the node is a leaf) can we consider removing the parent.\n\nThus steps:\n1. Build a mapping from node id to node dict, and child lists.\n2. Determine initial set of retained nodes: all non-removable nodes (including root) must be retained. Also any node that is a required symbol node? Actually required_symbols is a list of symbols that must appear in the tree. The root is always retained but its symbol is \"root\". The other required symbols might be on nodes that are removable? But if a node has a required symbol, it must be retained because we cannot remove it? The instruction says \"Never remove the root, a required symbol, or a node with retained children.\" So a node whose symbol is in required_symbols cannot be removed. So we mark such nodes as forced to retain.\n\n3. Process nodes in descending depth order. For ties, use node ID as tiebreaker (stable). For each node that is removable and not forced (symbol not required, and not root), check:\n   - Is it currently retained? (Initially all removable nodes are retained, but after removals we update.)\n   - Are all its children already removed (i.e., none of its children are currently retained)?\n   - Would removing it cause any required symbol to be lost? Compute the set of symbols of retained nodes after hypothetical removal: remove this node and its subtree. Since children are already removed or not, but we need to ensure that after removal, all required symbols still exist in the remaining retained nodes. This can be done by checking if any required symbol is only present in the subtree of this node (including the node itself). So we need to know, for each required symbol, which nodes provide it. If the node's subtree is the only provider of that required symbol, then we cannot remove it.\n   - If both conditions satisfied, remove the node: mark it as not retained.\n\n4. After processing all nodes, collect retained node ids (sorted) and removed node ids in the order they were removed (the actual removal sequence). The output must have \"removed_node_ids\" (array, order of removal) and \"retained_node_ids\" (sorted? The description says \"Return sorted retained IDs and the actual removal order.\" So retained_node_ids sorted, removed_node_ids in removal order.\n\nEdge cases: utility values are present but not used? In Perses, utility might be used to decide order among removable nodes? But the description says \"Consider deeper parse-tree nodes before shallower nodes.\" and \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So utility is not used. So we ignore utility.\n\nAlso \"grammar_valid\" and \"interestingness_preserved\" are constraints that are enforced by the conditions (required symbols). So we just need to ensure required symbols remain.\n\nNow implement solve(case) that takes payload dict with keys \"nodes\" and \"required_symbols\". Return dict with \"removed_node_ids\" and \"retained_node_ids\".\n\nImplementation details:\n- Build dict node_map: id -> node (including children list initially empty, and also we might want to store descendants symbol set? Could compute on the fly.)\n- Build parent mapping, children list.\n- Identify forced_retain: nodes with symbol in required_symbols or parent is None (root) or removable=False? Actually removable=False nodes must be retained. So forced_retain = set of nodes that are not removable (removable==False) OR symbol in required_symbols. Root is not removable by definition (removable=False).\n- Initially all nodes are retained except those we will remove.\n- We'll maintain a set \"retained\" of node ids. Initially all nodes that are forced_retain are retained, and also removable nodes are retained initially because we may choose to keep them. But we need to iterate and remove some.\n- However, note: nodes with removable=True can be removed only if conditions hold. So we add them to retained initially and then possibly remove.\n\n- We need to process in descending depth. For nodes with same depth, sort by id string (lexicographically? Use node id as is, it's a string like \"n1\", \"n2\". The instruction says \"Use node ID as the stable tie break at equal depth.\" So we sort by depth descending then by id ascending.\n\n- For each node in that order:\n   - If node id not in retained: skip (already removed)\n   - If node id in forced_retain: skip (cannot remove)\n   - Check if any child of this node is retained. If yes, skip (cannot remove because it would remove retained child)\n   - Check if required symbols are still present after removal. We need to compute if any required symbol is only present in this node's subtree. We can precompute for each node the set of symbols in its subtree (including itself). Then for each required symbol, if it appears only in this node's subtree (i.e., after removal the symbol would be missing), then cannot remove. To be efficient, for each symbol, we can count total occurrences across retained nodes. But easier: before removal, gather all symbols from retained nodes. After hypothetical removal (excluding this node's subtree), check if required_symbols subset of remaining symbols. Since removal only affects this node's subtree, we can compute set of symbols in retained nodes not in this node's subtree. But we need to know which nodes are in this subtree. We can precompute subtree node sets for each node (list of node ids in subtree). Then compute the symbols from retained nodes outside that subtree. Since we process nodes in descending depth, when we remove a node, its children might already be removed, but we still need to consider the entire subtree (including children that were already removed, they don't contribute). So we need to consider only currently retained nodes in the subtree. So we can compute the set of symbols in the subtree by traversing the tree only including nodes that are currently retained. But that would be O(n^2) if done per node. Simpler: We can maintain a count of each required symbol across retained nodes. Then for a candidate node, we subtract the symbol contributions from its subtree (only retained nodes in subtree) and check if counts still >=1 for all required. If yes, then removal is safe. After removal, we decrement counts for all nodes in its subtree (only retained ones). Since we process nodes in descending depth and remove them, we can update counts as we remove.\n\nSo strategy:\n- Precompute for each node a list of all nodes in its subtree (including itself). Since depth increases from root, we can compute by DFS: children first then parent. For tree with parent pointers, we can build children list then do a post-order traversal to collect subtree nodes. Also store for each node the set of symbols in its subtree? Actually we need to know symbols of retained nodes in subtree during the process. Since nodes may be removed beforehand, the subtree contribution changes. So we need to track dynamically.\n\nBetter: Maintain a dict \"symbol_count\" for required_symbols: how many retained nodes have that symbol. Also maintain a set of retained nodes. When we consider removing a node, we compute how many of its descendants (including itself) are still retained and contribute to each required symbol. If for any required symbol, the count in the subtree equals the total count (i.e., that symbol is only present in this subtree), then cannot remove. So we need to know the counts of required symbols in the subtree. We can precompute for each node the total symbols in its subtree (including all nodes, not just retained). But since some may be removed, we need dynamic. However, we can compute on the fly by traversing the subtree (only retained nodes) each time. Since n is small (maybe up to 10? The fixtures have 7 nodes), it's fine.\n\nAlternative: Since we remove nodes in descending depth, when we get to a parent, its children may already be removed. So we only need to consider the node itself and any retained children (which would be removable but not removed yet because we process deeper first? Actually we process deeper first, so children are processed before parent. So if a child was removable and met conditions, it would have been removed already. So when we consider parent, its children are either already removed (not retained) or they are forced retain (non-removable or required symbol). So the subtree of retained nodes under a parent is just the parent itself and possibly some forced-retain descendants. So we could precompute for each node the set of required symbols that appear in its subtree among forced-retain nodes. But that's manageable.\n\nGiven small n, simplest approach: For each candidate node, simulate removal by temporarily removing its subtree (all currently retained nodes in subtree) and check if all required symbols still present. We can compute this by iterating over retained nodes and building a set of symbols. Then for hypothetical removal, we subtract symbols from subtree and see if required still subset.\n\nImplementation steps:\n\n- Parse case: nodes list, required_symbols set.\n- Build node_map: id -> node (dict), also store children list after building parent links.\n- For each node, find children: iterate all nodes and add to parent's children list if parent not None.\n- Identify forced_retain_ids: set of ids where node['removable'] is False (including root) OR node['symbol'] in required_symbols. Note: root symbol is 'root', which may or may not be in required_symbols; but root is non-removable anyway.\n- Initialize retained_ids = set of all node ids (since all start retained). Actually we could start with all ids, but forced_retain will always remain. Others may be removed.\n- We'll maintain removed_order = [].\n- Build list of nodes sorted by depth descending, then id.\n- For each node_id in sorted list:\n    if node_id not in retained_ids: continue (already removed)\n    if node_id in forced_retain_ids: continue\n    node = node_map[node_id]\n    # Get all currently retained nodes in this node's subtree (including node)\n    # Compute subtree ids: We can precompute all descendant ids (including self) via DFS.\n    subtree_ids = get_subtree_ids(node_id) # precomputed set\n    retained_subtree = subtree_ids & retained_ids\n    # Compute symbols in retained_subtree:\n    subtree_symbols = set(node_map[nid]['symbol'] for nid in retained_subtree)\n    # Compute symbols in retained_ids outside subtree:\n    all_symbols = set(node_map[nid]['symbol'] for nid in retained_ids)\n    # Check if any required symbol is only in subtree:\n    if required_symbols.issubset(all_symbols - subtree_symbols): # after removal, still have all required\n        # Also check that node has no retained children? Actually if node has no retained children, then removal is okay. But if a child is retained (e.g., forced retain), then that child's subtree would be part of retained_subtree, and removal would remove that child, which violates \"never remove a node with retained children\". So we already capture that because if child is retained, then its id is in retained_subtree and will be removed. So we need to ensure that no retained child exists? But if child is forced retain, it cannot be removed. So if a forced-retain child is in subtree, then removal would remove that child, which is not allowed. So we should check: if any child in children_list is in retained_ids, then cannot remove that node (since that child would be removed). This is explicit in the rules. So we add condition: if any child of node (immediate children) is in retained_ids, skip.\n    # Additionally, we need to ensure that removing this node does not cause any required symbol to be missing from the remaining tree.\n    # So combine conditions:\n        # (a) no immediate child that is retained (because those would be removed)\n        # (b) required symbols still present after removal of subtree.\n    # If both satisfied, remove: remove node_id from retained_ids, append to removed_order, and for each nid in retained_subtree, remove them from retained_ids (since whole subtree is removed). But careful: If node has children that are not retained (already removed), they are not in retained_subtree. So fine.\n    # Since we remove the entire subtree, we can just do retained_ids -= subtree_ids (since subtree_ids includes node and all descendants regardless of retention status? Actually we only want to remove those that are currently retained. But if a descendant was already removed, it's not in retained_ids. So we can do retained_ids.difference_update(retained_subtree). But note: retained_subtree already includes only currently retained nodes, so we can just remove those.\n    # Alternative: simply remove node_id from retained_ids, and then when we process deeper nodes, we already removed children if they were removable and processed earlier. So we don't need to explicitly remove children again because they are already removed or they are forced retain (which we won't remove). But if we remove a parent, we must also remove its forced-retain children? The rule says \"Never remove ... a node with retained children.\" So if a parent has a forced-retain child (retained), we cannot remove the parent. So we already prevented that with the child check. So after we confirm no retained children, the children are either already removed or are not retained (which they wouldn't be because they would have been processed earlier and removed if removable). So the only children retained are forced-retain, and we skip. So when we remove a node, its children are already not in retained_ids (if they were removable) or are forced-retain (but we skip). So we only need to remove the node itself from retained_ids. However, what about grandchildren? If a child was removed earlier, the grandchildren are also removed. So we only need to remove the node itself. But consider scenario: node is removable, has a child that was not processed yet because depth same? Actually depth of child is larger (deeper), so it is processed before parent. So when we get to parent, child is either removed (if removable and conditions met) or retained (if forced retain). So we only need to remove the parent node itself; the entire subtree is already considered removed because children are gone. So we can simply do retained_ids.remove(node_id) and append to removed_order. That simplifies.\n\nBut we still need to check required symbols: we subtract node's symbol contribution (and the contributions of any retained descendants? Actually if children are not retained, they don't contribute. So subtree contribution is just the node itself and possibly forced-retain descendants? But forced-retain descendants would be retained, so if they exist, the node cannot be removed because child check would fail. So in the allowed case, the node's subtree has no retained nodes other than itself. So we only need to check that removing the node's own symbol does not cause a required symbol to be missing. So we can just check: for each required symbol, if the node's symbol equals that required symbol, and the count of that symbol in retained_ids is exactly 1, then removing it would lose the symbol. So we can maintain symbol_counts for required symbols. That's simpler.\n\nSo plan:\n\n- Build node_map, children lists.\n- forced_retain_ids set.\n- retained_ids = set of all node ids.\n- Compute symbol_counts for required_symbols: count of retained nodes with that symbol. Initialize by iterating all nodes.\n- removed_order = []\n- Sort nodes by depth descending, then id.\n- For each node_id in sorted:\n    if node_id not in retained_ids: continue\n    if node_id in forced_retain_ids: continue\n    node = node_map[node_id]\n    # Check if any immediate child is in retained_ids:\n    children = node_map[node_id].get('children', [])\n    has_retained_child = any(child_id in retained_ids for child_id in children)\n    if has_retained_child:\n        continue\n    # Check if required symbols still satisfied after removal:\n    symbol = node['symbol']\n    # If symbol is in required_symbols and its count == 1, then removal would lose that symbol.\n    if symbol in required_symbols and symbol_counts[symbol] == 1:\n        continue\n    # Also need to consider that children already removed may have contributed required symbols? But children already removed are not in retained_ids, so they don't contribute. So no issue.\n    # So safe to remove.\n    retained_ids.remove(node_id)\n    removed_order.append(node_id)\n    symbol_counts[symbol] -= 1  # decrement count for this symbol\n\n- After loop, retained_node_ids = sorted list of retained_ids (sorted by id? The spec expects sorted retained IDs; we can sort them lexicographically).\n- removed_node_ids = removed_order (list in removal order).\n- Return {\"removed_node_ids\": removed_node_ids, \"retained_node_ids\": retained_node_ids}.\n\nTest with provided fixtures? Need to verify.\n\nFixture b-000 payload: nodes with depths 0 root, 1 n1, n2, n3, 2 n4, n5, n6. required_symbols: [\"root\", \"s1\"]. Forced retain: root (removable false), n1 (symbol s1, required), n3? symbol s0 not required, removable false, so forced retain. n5 symbol s2, removable false, forced retain. n2 removable true, symbol s2 not required. n4 removable true, symbol s1 is required! n4 has symbol s1, which is required, so n4 is forced retain because symbol in required_symbols. n6 removable true, symbol s0 not required.\n\nSo forced_retain: root, n1, n3, n4, n5. Removable candidates: n2, n6.\n\nSort by depth descending: depth 2: n4 (forced), n5 (forced), n6 (removable). n6 processed first? After sorting depth descending, then id: n4, n5, n6? Actually ids: n4, n5, n6. n4 and n5 are forced, skip. n6: check children? n6 has no children (leaf). Has retained child? none. Symbol s0 not required. symbol_counts: s0 maybe appears in n3? n3 symbol s0, removable false, retained. So count of s0 is at least 1 (n3). Removing n6 does not affect required symbols (root, s1). So remove n6. retained_ids.remove(n6), removed_order append n6, symbol_counts[s0] decrement (from 2? n3 and n6, to 1).\n\nNext depth 1 nodes: n1 (forced), n2 (removable), n3 (forced). Process order: n1, n2, n3? Sort by id: n1, n2, n3. n1 forced skip. n2: check children? n2 has children n4. n4 is retained (forced). So has_retained_child = True => skip. n3 forced skip.\n\nSo after loop, removed nodes: [\"n6\"]. retained_ids: root, n1, n2, n3, n4, n5. But n2 is still retained. Is that expected? Let's think about algorithm: n2 has child n4 (required symbol s1) that is retained, so n2 cannot be removed because it would remove n4. So n2 remains. So output: removed_node_ids = [\"n6\"], retained_node_ids sorted = [\"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\"]? Actually sort ids: \"n1\",\"n2\",\"n3\",\"n4\",\"n5\",\"root\". That seems plausible.\n\nBut is there any other node that could be removed? n4 is forced, n5 forced, n1 forced, n3 forced. So only n6 removed.\n\nCheck fixture b-001: similar structure, but utility values differ but not used. So same outcome.\n\nb-002: same.\n\nThus algorithm seems fine.\n\nEdge: what if a removable node has no retained children but its removal would cause required symbol loss? e.g., if a node is the only provider of a required symbol, we keep it.\n\nAlso note: The root node is not removed, even if it had no required symbol? But root's removable is false anyway.\n\nNow implement solve(case) accordingly.\n\nPotential pitfalls:\n- Need to handle that case payload may have additional keys, but we only use nodes and required_symbols.\n- Ensure we return dict with correct keys as per output schema.\n\nNow produce final answer: one JSON object with \"implementation\" key containing Python source. Ensure proper escaping.\n\nLet's write code."},"logprobs":null,"finish_reason":"stop"}],"usage":{"prompt_tokens":2088,"completion_tokens":5212,"total_tokens":7300,"prompt_tokens_details":{"cached_tokens":2048},"completion_tokens_details":{"reasoning_tokens":4725},"prompt_cache_hit_tokens":2048,"prompt_cache_miss_tokens":40},"system_fingerprint":"fp_8b330d02d0_prod0820_fp8_kvcache_20260402"}